This is an ugly hack borrowed from AD&D. Essentially, the rule is this. 
Attributes range from 3-18 (for humans); scores higher than 18 are 
reserved for giants, titans, gods, etc. However, TSR decided that in 
the case of strength, there was too much of a gap between 18 (ogre 
strength) and 19 (hill giant strength), so they added "exceptional 
strength": if your strength is 18 and you are a fighter, you roll 1d100 
and tack that number onto your strength, as 18/NN, where NN is a two 
digit number. 18/100 is a special case notationally, as it's often 
written as 18/00. 18/00 is the highest strength a human can achieve by 
normal means. 

If this whole thing sounds stupid, you're right, it is. They dropped 
the whole mess in third edition.

On top of all that, NetHack ignores the AD&D definition of what 
attributes greater than 18 mean and allows obtaining them through 
simple magical items. Angband adopted a definition which says that 
scores above 18 never happen, but every 10 points after the / are 
equivalent to a point of real attribute; thus, 18/05 would be a 19, 
18/55 would be a 24, etc., and I believe NetHack uses something similar 
internally. So the mapping from 'displayed score' to 'actual score' 
would be something like:
  [3,18]         n -> n
  [18/01,18/99]  18/n -> 19+floor(n/10)
  18/00          28
  [19,25]        n -> 10+n

